  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with a weak ridge poking up from\nColorado through Wyoming and into eastern Montana. Far to the \nWest a closed low has begun moving onto the shore of the Pacific \nNorthwest. In between these two airmasses SW flow has set up in \nbetween pulling in pacific moisture from off the coast of northern\nCalifornia into Idaho and western Montana. \n\nToday through this evening: Ridge exiting to the west will keep\nconditions stable through most of the day and therefore dry.\nHowever as the closed low to the west moves inland over the PCNW a\nsurface  pressure gradient will form creating a strong wind that \nwill easily lead to a Lake Wind Advisory this afternoon. \n\nMonday morning to evening: While the stability in the upper \natmosphere will keep conditions dry into the early evening. The \nexit of the trough and entrance of SW flow will allow enough \ninstability and pacific moisture to strike off some scattered \nrain showers and embedded thunderstorms around midnight lasting through\nMonday afternoon. Cold front associated with the trough will \nlikely pass across the area from midnight to 6AM and produce \nprecipitation totals ranging between 0.01\" and 0.25\". The front \nitself has backed up 6 to 12 hours from the previous forecast so \ntheir is lower confidence in the timing of the passage and \nassociated showers. \n\nWind will veer westerly behind the front Monday morning. This \nwill of comparable in strength to today in reaching advisory \ncriteria. Wave heights have the potential to reach 4.5 ft, \nparticularly Monday afternoon.\n\nTuesday onward: The trough will fully exit the region Tuesday \nmorning with the zonal flow left in its wake. Slight chances for \nrain showers are possible Tuesday night into Wednesday morning due\nto a swath of pacific moisture moving throughout. Confidence in \nrain amounts are low. Rain chances diminish Wednesday afternoon \nas skies clear and temperatures warm under a developing ridge over\nthe PCNW Thursday. A weak trough has embedded itself between us \nand the ridge in a significant portion of the ensembles which is \nlowering confidence heavily from Thursday onward. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNo Deviation from NBM were observed.\n\nThere is HIGH confidence in sustained winds at or above 20 mph on\nSunday and Monday across Fort Peck Lake.\n\nThere is MODERATE confidence timing of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Monday morning,(They could begin earlier or later) \nbut there HIGH confidence in them lasting into the evenings \nhours.\n\nThere is LOW confidence in the forecast beyond Thursday.\n\nDON/GAH",
